ZTEXT001000009ec7d2167670000000
Original Zero text format and native reader

tools/text.zero is original Zero source for zero-text on Linux and
zero-text.exe on Windows. It uses native Zero file operations and no external
codec, process, script or interpreter.

Commands
  zero-text make INPUT NEW_ZTEXT
  zero-text read FILE
  zero-text verify FILE
  zero-text selftest

make wraps an existing UTF-8 source as our .ztext format. It refuses an existing
destination, flushes the new file, closes it, reads it back and compares every
byte and exact EOF. If writing or verification fails, it removes only its own
new incomplete output. Inputs and existing destinations are preserved.
read validates the entire container before outputting any payload bytes.
verify validates silently and returns success only for a complete valid file.
The maximum payload is 1048576 bytes; empty payloads are valid.
selftest runs 51 original-Zero cases through the same production decoder: UTF-8
boundaries, overlong encodings, surrogates, invalid/truncated continuations, NUL,
malformed header fields, checksum/length errors, partial files and 1MiB bounds.
Case numbers are displayed from one. Exit0 means all passed; exit1 means failure.

ZTEXT001 wire contract
The header is exactly 32 bytes:
  bytes 0..7: ASCII ZTEXT001
  bytes 8..15: payload byte length, exactly eight lowercase hexadecimal digits
  bytes 16..23: payload FNV1a32 checksum, eight lowercase hexadecimal digits
  bytes 24..30: seven ASCII zero digits
  byte 31: LF (10)
Only the UTF-8 payload follows; no trailing bytes are permitted. FNV1a32 starts
at 2166136261, XORs each payload byte, then multiplies by 16777619 modulo 2^32.
This detects accidental corruption; it is not an authenticity signature.
UTF-8 is decoded as Unicode scalar values: overlong encodings, isolated or
missing continuation bytes, surrogates, values above U+10FFFF, and NUL are
rejected. Tab, carriage return, newline and other non-NUL ASCII are accepted.
The format adds no implicit newline and does not normalize Unicode or CR/LF.

The payload Hello followed by LF has byte length 00000006 and checksum
81218953. An empty payload has length 00000000 and checksum 811c9dc5.

Status codes: 0 success; 64 usage/unsupported host; 65 malformed text or limit;
66 input missing/unreadable; 70 allocation; 73 destination already exists or
cannot be created; 74 short/changing input, output flush, or read-back failure.
Diagnostic text is emitted only on failure. Native tools use at most about
2 MiB of mapped working memory and byte-preserving output.
